course: dictionary
lesson: 9164
title: Words with two meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one the writer means. The word snowflake is a good example. «Snowflakes have six sides or six arms.» Here a snowflake is a single piece of snow, made of tiny ice crystals.
explain: Look at the words around it. In «In cold clouds, raindrops usually start as ice», the clouds and the ice point to the science meaning. The insult meaning is slang, and it is unkind, so only use it with care.
explain: Check your choice by swapping in your meaning. Does «A lacy snowflake drifts down slowly» still make sense with a piece of snow? If it does, you have probably found the right meaning.
fact: A snowflake is a single piece of snow made of ice crystals, usually with six points or sides.
fact: In cold clouds, snowflakes form when water vapour freezes.
fact: «Big, complicated snowflakes are almost certainly all different», but very simple tiny crystals can look identical.
fact: «A hailstone is a solid ball of ice and pelts down»; a snowflake drifts down slowly.
fact: Snowflake is also slang, used as an insult for someone said to be too easily upset. This meaning is unkind.
exercise: «Snowflakes have six sides or six arms.» How many sides do snowflakes usually have? | *Six | Ten | Four | Eight
exercise: «A lacy snowflake drifts down slowly, but a hailstone is a solid ball of ice and pelts down.» What is the difference? | A snowflake pelts down, but a hailstone drifts | *A snowflake drifts slowly, but a hailstone pelts down | Both are warm and soft | Both are made of paper
exercise: Which meaning of snowflake fits a science lesson about clouds? | A person who is easily upset | A type of thunder | A paper decoration cut in class | *A single piece of snow made of ice crystals
test: «Big, complicated snowflakes are almost certainly all different.» What does the writer say about them? | They are all exactly the same | They are always melted | *They are almost certainly all different | They have only one point
test: Someone calls a classmate a snowflake as an insult. Which meaning is being used? | *A person said to be too easily upset | A single piece of snow | A paper craft from class | A type of hailstone
exam: What is the best way to check you have chosen the right meaning of a word with two meanings? | Pick the longest meaning | Look only at the first letter of the word | Ignore the sentence and guess | *Swap your meaning into the sentence and see if it still makes sense
